 Here  n = j − 1 n=j-1  for the power sequences, and  n = j n=j  for the Catalan and
Fuss–Catalan sequences. Agreement of finitely many terms of course does not
establish Wilf equivalence, but it seems unlikely that any sequences in these
data that agree up to seven occurrences will split later. 
 
 
 Although we have not analyzed this exhaustively, it seems that for
most of these apparent Wilf classes (sets of pattern-overlap
combinations with the same enumeration) the posets in question are
isomorphic, apart from the number and placement of elements that
belong to head or tail, or else cover and are covered by a single
element, and are comparable to all elements of the poset. Such
elements don’t affect the number of linear extensions. One exception
to this is the triple of patterns 125634 and 163425 with overlap 2 and
167359248 with overlap 3 (see
Figure  5.6 ), and it would be interesting
to find further such nontrivial Wilf classes. 
 
 
 8.   As mentioned in the footnote to item  7 , if
two pattern-overlap combinations have different enumeration for 1-7
occurrences, for patterns of length up to 12, then they have different
enumeration already for five occurrences. Is it possible that 5 is a
cutoff for any pair of patterns, of arbitrary lengths, or that there
is at least a small such bound that depends only on pattern length? 
 
 
 
 9.   The Wilf class with enumeration sequence  2 j − 1 2^{j-1}  contains,
among an apparent infinity of pattern-overlap combinations, 132546-2,
1324657-2 and 123584679-4. All can be explained by straightforward
arguments showing that on each pair of successive lines of the stack
there is a pair of elements that are incomparable to each other, but
otherwise all elements are comparable. All pattern posets we have
seen with this enumeration consist of cycles where two such
incomparable elements are the only deviation from the poset being a
chain. Similar posets seem to exist for the sequence  k n k^{n}  for any
positive  k k , and for an infinite collection of pattern-overlap
combinations in each case. 
 
 
 It seems plausible that understanding some of these Wilf equivalences
will lead to greater understanding than just for each class. For
